\name{lexdiv_profile}
\alias{lexdiv_spec}
\alias{lexdiv_grid}
\alias{lexdiv_plan}
\alias{lexdiv_methods}
\alias{lexdiv_presets}
\alias{lexdiv_profile}
\alias{lexdiv_profile_batch}
\alias{lexdiv_screen}
\alias{print.lexdiv_profile_results}
\alias{print.lexdiv_profile_batch_results}
\title{Build and Evaluate Explicit Lexical-Diversity Method Plans}
\usage{
lexdiv_spec(method_id, parameters = list(), request_id = NULL)

lexdiv_grid(
  method_id,
  parameter,
  values,
  request_id_prefix = NULL,
  max_values = 128L
)

lexdiv_plan(
  presets = "canonical",
  specs = list(),
  grids = list(),
  max_specs = 128L
)

lexdiv_methods()

lexdiv_presets()

lexdiv_profile(tokens, plan = lexdiv_plan())

lexdiv_profile_batch(
  documents,
  plan = lexdiv_plan(),
  id_col = "document_id",
  tokens_col = "tokens",
  max_rows = 1e6
)

lexdiv_screen(
  x,
  floors = c(tokens_50 = 50L, tokens_100 = 100L),
  max_rows = 2e+06
)
}
\arguments{
  \item{method_id}{One frozen method identifier returned by
  \code{lexdiv_methods()}. A method fixes its formula, scale, boundary, tail,
  and aggregation rules.}
  \item{parameters}{A plain named list of user-settable method parameters.
  Omitted values are materialized from the frozen defaults.}
  \item{request_id}{An optional plain ASCII identifier used to join one request
  to its profile result.}
  \item{parameter}{The one user-settable scalar parameter admitted by the
  selected v0.1 method.}
  \item{values}{A non-empty numeric vector whose elements become distinct
  method specifications after validation and normalization.}
  \item{request_id_prefix}{An optional ASCII prefix for the one-based request
  IDs generated by \code{lexdiv_grid()}.}
  \item{max_values}{A positive integer bound on values expanded eagerly by one
  grid call. Raise it explicitly only when that allocation is intentional.}
  \item{presets}{A duplicate-free plain character vector selected from
  \code{lexdiv_presets()}. Use \code{character()} for a custom-only plan.}
  \item{specs}{One \code{lexdiv_spec}, or a plain list of such objects, appended
  after preset requests.}
  \item{grids}{One \code{lexdiv_grid}, or a plain list of such objects, appended
  after explicit specifications.}
  \item{max_specs}{A positive integer bound on the number of distinct normalized
  specifications in a plan.}
  \item{tokens}{One pre-tokenized document accepted by
  \code{lexdiv_metrics()}.}
  \item{plan}{A normalized request plan returned by \code{lexdiv_plan()}.}
  \item{documents}{A named list or data frame/list-column input accepted by
  \code{lexdiv_metrics_batch()}.}
  \item{id_col}{The explicit document-ID column name for data-frame input.}
  \item{tokens_col}{The token-vector list-column name for data-frame input.}
  \item{max_rows}{A positive integer output bound, checked before computation or
  allocation. For \code{lexdiv_profile_batch()} it bounds
  document-by-specification rows; for \code{lexdiv_screen()} it bounds
  source-row-by-floor rows.}
  \item{x}{A result returned by \code{lexdiv_profile()} or
  \code{lexdiv_profile_batch()}.}
  \item{floors}{A non-empty named numeric vector of positive integer token-count
  screens. Names become \code{screen_id}; values become
  \code{minimum_tokens}.}
}
\value{
\code{lexdiv_spec()} returns a normalized \code{lexdiv_spec};
\code{lexdiv_grid()} returns an ordered list of specifications;
\code{lexdiv_plan()} returns a deduplicated \code{lexdiv_plan} with an MD5
fingerprint over normalized request identity and order.

\code{lexdiv_methods()} returns a metadata data frame with human-readable
labels and definitions, conventional within-method direction, contract scale,
exact method identity, parameters, and advisory token floors.
\code{lexdiv_presets()} returns a preset metadata data frame.
\code{lexdiv_profile()} returns one core metric row per plan request with a
separate profile envelope. \code{lexdiv_profile_batch()} adds the existing batch
envelope and returns document-major, request-minor long form.

\code{lexdiv_screen()} returns a \code{lexdiv_screen_results} data frame with one
row per source result and named floor. It deliberately has no metric-value column.
}
\description{
Builds bounded, reproducible plans for computing multiple parameter settings of
the frozen lexical-diversity methods without changing the established canonical
API. The identity hierarchy is metric family, frozen method, and normalized
method-plus-parameter specification.
}
\details{
The \code{canonical} preset contains the eleven frozen methods at their default
parameters. The \code{length_50_100} preset contains those eleven requests plus
MSTTR at segment length 100 and MATTR at window length 100. It has thirteen
specifications: MTLD is computed only once because a 50- versus 100-token quality
screen does not change its calculation.

Duplicate normalized method/parameter specifications from presets, explicit
specifications, or grids collapse at their first position. Distinct remaining
specifications must have distinct request IDs. Parameter grids form an ordered
union, not a cross-product across metrics. Requested limits are checked before a
large result is allocated. Independently of \code{max_specs}, a plan rejects more
than 1,024 raw preset, explicit-specification, and expanded-grid candidates
before normalization; this prevents an arbitrarily large duplicate input from
bypassing the distinct-specification bound.

Profile rows make the method's advisory default floor explicit as
\code{default_quality_floor_tokens}. Independently requested screens are
non-censoring: they do not change a metric value, effective parameter, status, or
document membership. A 50- or 100-token screen is therefore not an MTLD method
variant, and truncating a document to one of those lengths would be a separate
sampling design. Passing a screen does not establish measurement validity,
reliability, proficiency, or writing quality; failing one does not invalidate an
otherwise computable metric row.

The profile and screen schemas are distinct from the core metric and batch
schemas. Third-party implementation names are not compatibility presets; such a
preset requires an exact, version-pinned method contract and boundary fixtures.
}
\examples{
methods <- lexdiv_methods()
lexdiv_presets()
mattr_method <- methods$method_id[methods$metric_id == "mattr"]

mattr_25 <- lexdiv_spec(
  mattr_method,
  parameters = list(window_length = 25),
  request_id = "mattr_25"
)

grid <- lexdiv_grid(
  mattr_method,
  parameter = "window_length",
  values = c(50, 100),
  request_id_prefix = "mattr"
)
plan <- lexdiv_plan(presets = character(), specs = mattr_25, grids = grid)

tokens <- rep(c("a", "b", "a", "c", "d"), 25)
profile <- lexdiv_profile(tokens, plan)
profile[, c("request_id", "metric_id", "value", "status")]

screens <- lexdiv_screen(profile)
screens[, c("request_id", "screen_id", "minimum_tokens", "passes_screen")]

documents <- list(document_a = tokens, document_b = rev(tokens))
batch_profile <- lexdiv_profile_batch(documents, plan)
head(batch_profile[, c("document_id", "request_id", "metric_id", "status")])

all_lengths <- lexdiv_plan("length_50_100")
length(all_lengths$specifications)
}
